.TH INVF-ABSORB 8 "2026-11" "InvariantFS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
invf-absorb \- adopt an existing filesystem inside a new InvariantFS volume
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B invf-absorb
.B plan
.I image reserve.list
.br
.B invf-absorb
.B publish
.I volume name reference reserve.list prefix_blocks
.br
.B invf-absorb
.B verify
.I volume name reference
.br
.B invf-absorb
.B commit
.I image|device reserve.list
.RB [ \-\-out\-name=\fINAME\fR]
.RB [ \-\-packs=\fIDIR\fR]
.RB [ \-\-mkfs=\fIPATH\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B invf-absorb
turns an existing filesystem into an InvariantFS volume that keeps it as a file
at the root of the new volume, byte for byte. After
.BR "invf-absorb commit" ,
mounting the volume shows the old filesystem as
.I old.ext4
(or
.I old.xfs
for XFS, or whatever the containerpack's
.B absorb_name
says), extract it with
.BR invf\-cat (1)
and the original checker still opens it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
invf-absorb commit part.ext4 reserve.list
invf-cat part.ext4 old.ext4 > old.img
e2fsck \-fn old.img
.EE
.RE
.PP
Nothing here knows what ext4 or XFS is. A containerpack declares that its images
are filesystems
.RB ( "absorb = 1" ),
gives the name to publish, and provides the
.B classify
command that says which bytes the filesystem uses; the tool asks, and refuses
when no pack claims the image.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B plan
Probe the image and write the reserve list: one
.I "<start_block> <count>"
line per range the filesystem uses. Writes nothing to the image.
.TP
.B commit
The whole in\-place adoption. It stages the used bytes OUTSIDE the device, saves
the prefix that
.BR invf\-mkfs (8)
will overwrite, builds the volume with those ranges RESERVED, publishes the old
filesystem inside it, and verifies the read\-back. It refuses before writing
anything when a precondition does not hold: no pack claims the image, the image
has no prefix, or there is not enough free space for the staged copy.
.PP
Staging is not an optimisation. Reading the reserved ranges from the target while
the framed copy lands in the target's own free space destroys source bytes that
have not been read yet, because a filesystem that fills its device has its free
space interleaved with its used ranges.
.TP
.B publish
Publish a filesystem from an offline reference into a volume already built with
.BR "mkfs \-\-reserve" .
.TP
.B verify
Read the published file back and compare it against the reference, in chunks.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-out\-name= NAME
Name to publish under (default: the pack's
.BR absorb_name ).
.TP
.BI \-\-packs= DIR
Containerpack directory (default: the repository's
.IR tools/codecpacks ,
or next to the binary).
.TP
.BI \-\-mkfs= PATH
The
.B invf\-mkfs
to run (default: the sibling binary).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when the operation refused or failed, with the reason on standard
error; 2 on usage errors. A refusal never leaves a partly written volume: the
tool checks every precondition before it writes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <target>.absorb\-stage
The staged copy of the used bytes, removed on success. KEPT when
.BR invf\-mkfs (8)
fails after it started, because at that point it is the only intact copy of the
source.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR invf\-mkfs (8),
.BR invf\-convert (8),
.BR invf\-cat (1),
.BR invf\-fsck (8),
.BR invarifs (7)
